1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:01,436
1803.

2
00:00:01,385 --> 00:00:03,007
Refrigerators don't exist.

3
00:00:03,136 --> 00:00:04,758
How do you carry a vaccine across

4
00:00:04,569 --> 00:00:06,111
the ocean without it spoiling?

5
00:00:06,526 --> 00:00:08,320
The Balmis expedition's answer:

6
00:00:08,338 --> 00:00:09,513
inside an arm.

7
00:00:09,641 --> 00:00:11,963
Twenty-two boys from the foundling home in

8
00:00:11,774 --> 00:00:13,545
Corunna form a living chain.

9
00:00:13,978 --> 00:00:15,795
One child is vaccinated.

10
00:00:15,923 --> 00:00:18,406
Days later, the vaccine passes from his

11
00:00:18,217 --> 00:00:19,254
arm to the next.

12
00:00:19,387 --> 00:00:21,467
And so on, arm to arm, for

13
00:00:21,279 --> 00:00:22,236
the whole voyage.

14
00:00:22,654 --> 00:00:24,643
They are cared for by Isabel Zendal,

15
00:00:24,730 --> 00:00:26,134
head of the foundling home.

16
00:00:26,554 --> 00:00:30,665
The Canaries, Puerto Rico, Venezuela, Cuba, Mexico,

17
00:00:30,740 --> 00:00:31,800
the Philippines…

18
00:00:31,887 --> 00:00:33,084
all the way to China.

19
00:00:33,213 --> 00:00:36,348
In Venezuela alone, more than twelve thousand

20
00:00:36,160 --> 00:00:38,264
people vaccinated in under a month.

21
00:00:38,686 --> 00:00:40,595
And when they left, they set up

22
00:00:40,406 --> 00:00:42,349
vaccination boards to carry on.

23
00:00:42,478 --> 00:00:45,247
Medical historians call it the first international

24
00:00:45,058 --> 00:00:47,081
vaccination campaign in history.

25
00:00:47,506 --> 00:00:49,472
Share it with someone who thinks logistics

26
00:00:49,284 --> 00:00:50,080
is boring.

